## Power sources: the name, how it powers, the description.
supply-battery-9v = 9 V battery
supply-battery-9v-phrase = with electrodes on a 9 V battery
supply-battery-9v-description-novice = A rectangular battery with two snap terminals on top. It gives 9 volts, but not for long: when the charge runs out, there is no more current.
supply-battery-9v-description-school = Inside are six 1.5 V cells. An alkaline battery holds ≈ 550 mA·h: for example, 0.55 A for an hour. Under load its voltage sags a little because of the internal resistance.
supply-usb-charger = USB charger (5 V)
supply-usb-charger-phrase = with electrodes on a USB charger
supply-usb-charger-description-novice = An ordinary USB port gives 5 volts. Phones are powered and charged through it.
supply-usb-charger-description-school = A standard USB 2.0 port gives up to 2.5 W, that is up to 0.5 A at 5 V; a larger current trips its protection.
supply-bench = Laboratory power supply 0–30 V
supply-bench-phrase = with electrodes on a laboratory power supply
supply-bench-supply-description-novice = A device with a knob to set any voltage from 0 to 30 volts. It shows how much current flows.
supply-bench-supply-description-school = The supply holds the set voltage, and if the current exceeds 5 A it limits it. Voltages up to 30–40 V are safe for experiments.
